{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WpXLaNfv5W7vY5eZxt7zwG","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd030-md5sum\n     2→title: \"cmd/md5sum: Compute and Check MD5 Message Digests\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  macOS ships md5 rather than md5sum; the output format differs from GNU md5sum. Scripts\n     6→  that generate or verify checksums using the GNU md5sum format cannot use the macOS\n     7→  native binary. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary\n     8→  (gmd5sum, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, using\n     9→  crypto/md5 from the Go standard library, verified by differential testing via\n    10→  pkg/testutils.\n    11→\n    12→goals:\n    13→  - G1: Compute MD5 digests for one or more files or stdin and print them in GNU format, matching gmd5sum behavior.\n    14→  - G2: Support --check (-c) to verify a checksum file and report mismatches.\n    15→  - G3: Support --binary (-b) and --text (-t) modes and the BSD-compatible --tag output format.\n    16→\n    17→requirements:\n    18→  R1:\n    19→    title: Digest computation\n    20→    items:\n    21→      - R1.1: Must compute the MD5 digest of each named file and write one line per file in the format \"HASH  FILENAME\" (two spaces for text mode, one space and asterisk \"HASH *FILENAME\" for binary mode). The default is text mode on non-Windows systems.\n    22→      - R1.2: Must read from stdin and print \"HASH  -\" when no file arguments are given or when \"-\" is provided as a filename.\n    23→      - R1.3: --tag must use BSD-style output format \"MD5 (FILENAME) = HASH\" instead of the default GNU format.\n    24→      - R1.4: Must exit 1 when any named file cannot be opened, printing an error to stderr, and continue processing remaining files.\n    25→\n    26→  R2:\n    27→    title: Checksum verification (--check, -c)\n    28→    items:\n    29→      - R2.1: \"--check FILE must read FILE line by line, parsing lines in GNU format (HASH  FILENAME or HASH *FILENAME) or BSD tag format, compute the digest for each named file, and compare it to the stored hash.\"\n    30→      - R2.2: 'For each verified file, must print \"FILENAME: OK\" or \"FILENAME: FAILED\" to stdout. After processing all entries, if any failed, must exit 1.'\n    31→      - R2.3: --warn (-w) must print a warning to stderr for each improperly formatted line in the checksum file; without --warn, malformed lines are silently skipped.\n    32→      - R2.4: --quiet suppresses the \"OK\" lines; only failures are printed. --status suppresses all output; exit code conveys success/failure.\n    33→\n    34→  R3:\n    35→    title: Binary and text mode flags\n    36→    items:\n    37→      - R3.1: -b (--binary) marks the input as binary; the output line uses one space followed by an asterisk before the filename (\"HASH *FILENAME\"). On Linux/macOS there is no functional difference between binary and text mode for digest computation.\n    38→      - R3.2: -t (--text) is the default; the output line uses two spaces before the filename (\"HASH  FILENAME\").\n    39→      - R3.3: When --tag is given with -b, the output is BSD tag format; the mode flag has no effect on the tag format.\n    40→\n    41→  R4:\n    42→    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    43→    items:\n    44→      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all files are processed and all verified digests match.\n    45→      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any file cannot be opened, any digest fails verification, or the checksum file is unreadable.\n    46→      - R4.3: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n    47→\n    48→non_goals:\n    49→  - cmd/md5sum does not implement HMAC-MD5 or keyed variants; it computes raw MD5 digests only.\n    50→  - cmd/md5sum does not implement the --strict flag (exit non-zero for improperly formatted lines); --warn is sufficient.\n    51→\n    52→acceptance_criteria:\n    53→  - \"AC1: echo -n 'abc' | md5sum produces 'md5sum output matching gmd5sum', verified byte-for-byte.\"\n    54→  - \"AC2: md5sum on a known file produces the correct MD5 hash in GNU format, matching gmd5sum.\"\n    55→  - \"AC3: md5sum --check on a valid checksum file exits 0 and prints 'FILENAME: OK' for each entry.\"\n    56→  - \"AC4: md5sum --check on a checksum file with one mismatch exits 1 and prints 'FILENAME: FAILED'.\"\n    57→  - \"AC5: Differential test against gmd5sum passes for all required test cases.\"\n    58→  - \"AC6: cmd/md5sum compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    59→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a39c2daa-64d0-494a-8cfd-c05281f49680","uuid":"f2dc3e84-f87d-4d12-9beb-73752d2a8aa0","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/375/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd030-md5sum.yaml","content":"id: prd030-md5sum\ntitle: \"cmd/md5sum: Compute and Check MD5 Message Digests\"\n\nproblem: |\n  macOS ships md5 rather than md5sum; the output format differs from GNU md5sum. Scripts\n  that generate or verify checksums using the GNU md5sum format cannot use the macOS\n  native binary. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary\n  (gmd5sum, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, using\n  crypto/md5 from the Go standard library, verified by differential testing via\n  pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Compute MD5 digests for one or more files or stdin and print them in GNU format, matching gmd5sum behavior.\n  - G2: Support --check (-c) to verify a checksum file and report mismatches.\n  - G3: Support --binary (-b) and --text (-t) modes and the BSD-compatible --tag output format.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Digest computation\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must compute the MD5 digest of each named file and write one line per file in the format \"HASH  FILENAME\" (two spaces for text mode, one space and asterisk \"HASH *FILENAME\" for binary mode). The default is text mode on non-Windows systems.\n      - R1.2: Must read from stdin and print \"HASH  -\" when no file arguments are given or when \"-\" is provided as a filename.\n      - R1.3: --tag must use BSD-style output format \"MD5 (FILENAME) = HASH\" instead of the default GNU format.\n      - R1.4: Must exit 1 when any named file cannot be opened, printing an error to stderr, and continue processing remaining files.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Checksum verification (--check, -c)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: \"--check FILE must read FILE line by line, parsing lines in GNU format (HASH  FILENAME or HASH *FILENAME) or BSD tag format, compute the digest for each named file, and compare it to the stored hash.\"\n      - R2.2: 'For each verified file, must print \"FILENAME: OK\" or \"FILENAME: FAILED\" to stdout. After processing all entries, if any failed, must exit 1.'\n      - R2.3: --warn (-w) must print a warning to stderr for each improperly formatted line in the checksum file; without --warn, malformed lines are silently skipped.\n      - R2.4: --quiet suppresses the \"OK\" lines; only failures are printed. --status suppresses all output; exit code conveys success/failure.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Binary and text mode flags\n    items:\n      - R3.1: -b (--binary) marks the input as binary; the output line uses one space followed by an asterisk before the filename (\"HASH *FILENAME\"). On Linux/macOS there is no functional difference between binary and text mode for digest computation.\n      - R3.2: -t (--text) is the default; the output line uses two spaces before the filename (\"HASH  FILENAME\").\n      - R3.3: When --tag is given with -b, the output is BSD tag format; the mode flag has no effect on the tag format.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all files are processed and all verified digests match.\n      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any file cannot be opened, any digest fails verification, or the checksum file is unreadable.\n      - R4.3: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/md5sum does not implement HMAC-MD5 or keyed variants; it computes raw MD5 digests only.\n  - cmd/md5sum does not implement the --strict flag (exit non-zero for improperly formatted lines); --warn is sufficient.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: echo -n 'abc' | md5sum produces 'md5sum output matching gmd5sum', verified byte-for-byte.\"\n  - \"AC2: md5sum on a known file produces the correct MD5 hash in GNU format, matching gmd5sum.\"\n  - \"AC3: md5sum --check on a valid checksum file exits 0 and prints 'FILENAME: OK' for each entry.\"\n  - \"AC4: md5sum --check on a checksum file with one mismatch exits 1 and prints 'FILENAME: FAILED'.\"\n  - \"AC5: Differential test against gmd5sum passes for all required test cases.\"\n  - \"AC6: cmd/md5sum compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":59,"startLine":1,"totalLines":59}}}
